Day team for having me here.

00:02:52.007 --> 00:02:54.434
Um, I'm Steve Jones.

00:02:54.454 --> 00:03:00.106
I'm owner and CTO at Equalize Digital,
and I am pleased to be here today to

00:03:00.106 --> 00:03:06.223
present to you this talk on when does
page zoom become an accessibility failure?

00:03:10.842 --> 00:03:15.193
So I wanna start by setting the stage
a little bit and posing a question.

00:03:16.633 --> 00:03:20.777
So you're, you're an auditor or you're
just somebody that's looking to test their

00:03:20.778 --> 00:03:28.649
website, um, that maybe isn't a, a formal
auditor, but you zoom your page to 200%.

00:03:29.124 --> 00:03:33.753
The button text gets clipped, the
layout shifts, something looks

00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:38.699
off, and you ask yourself, "Is
that an accessibility failure?"

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:47.850
And that's exactly what we're gonna
cover here today, is getting more clarity

00:03:47.852 --> 00:03:54.791
around when, uh, webpage zoom actually
becomes an accessibility failure.

00:03:56.102 --> 00:04:03.463
So what we'll cover is what WCAG actually
requires at 200% and 400% zoom, common

00:04:03.463 --> 00:04:09.916
misconceptions about zoom testing, and
we will go over a practical framework

00:04:09.971 --> 00:04:12.787
for evaluating zoom issues consistently.

00:04:14.063 --> 00:04:16.332
So I wanna give a little bit
of background about myself.

00:04:16.366 --> 00:04:18.519
Um, I'm developer first.

00:04:18.637 --> 00:04:24.387
Um, while I make software that audits
websites for accessibility, such as

00:04:24.387 --> 00:04:29.303
our accessibility checker plugin,
I'm not primarily an auditor, and

00:04:29.307 --> 00:04:32.859
this talk is going to be kind of
pretty heavily on the auditor side.

00:04:33.469 --> 00:04:38.619
But it's gonna go deep into how a,
a team, an accessibility team, works

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.096
together from auditing to remediation.

00:04:42.051 --> 00:04:45.861
So the reason why I came up with this
talk, and the reason why I came up

00:04:45.863 --> 00:04:51.320
with this framework, was because of
inconsistencies in our own process, uh,

00:04:51.467 --> 00:04:57.177
uh, when it came to, uh, considering
zoom, uh, and remediation work.

00:04:57.999 --> 00:05:04.154
So I consulted with my team i- in
regards to this, and I, I kinda pushed

00:05:04.155 --> 00:05:08.049
the team to kinda think about this
deeply and to come up with a way to

00:05:08.086 --> 00:05:11.315
add more consistency in this flagging

00:05:14.271 --> 00:05:17.323
So let's take a little bit of
a step back and kind of look at

00:05:17.332 --> 00:05:20.150
why people zoom webpages anyway.

00:05:20.762 --> 00:05:29.535
And, and there are many reasons and,
and some of those are low vision, um,

00:05:30.183 --> 00:05:37.165
eye issues related to aging, fatigue,
um, uh, small or high-resolution

00:05:37.166 --> 00:05:40.943
screens that may require… You know,
if you're running a 4K monitor, maybe

00:05:40.945 --> 00:05:42.736
you need to increase it a little bit.

00:05:43.952 --> 00:05:49.235
But some- it's not always, uh,
uh, due to, um, you know, ability.

00:05:49.236 --> 00:05:51.556
Sometimes it's just
purely preference, right?

00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:55.085
Your reading preference, and
sometimes it's just more pleasant

00:05:55.091 --> 00:05:58.055
to zoom the website in so that
you can get a closer look at the

00:05:58.055 --> 00:06:00.677
text and that it's easier to read.

00:06:01.451 --> 00:06:04.149
Um, zoom is a built-in browser feature.

00:06:04.202 --> 00:06:06.035
It's not really an edge case here.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:10.321
It's not, uh, necessarily assistive
technology, but it's a feature of

00:06:10.321 --> 00:06:15.968
the browser, and us as auditors and
remediators should be mindful of

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.557
the implications of browser zoom.

00:06:23.161 --> 00:06:26.330
So when it comes to browser zoom,
there's typically two different

00:06:26.335 --> 00:06:28.121
things that people conflate.

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:34.895
So browser zoom, which scales
everything, text, images, layout,

00:06:34.979 --> 00:06:36.768
they all get scaled together.

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:40.082
And then there's text-only resize.

00:06:40.173 --> 00:06:45.686
This is typically an older browser
setting that scaled only text,

00:06:45.782 --> 00:06:48.126
leaving the layout un- untouched.

00:06:49.554 --> 00:06:56.789
The WCAG success criterion 1.4.4 was
written primarily when text zoom was

00:06:56.801 --> 00:07:03.011
the norm, but modern browsers are now,
uh, they default to full page zoom.

00:07:03.813 --> 00:07:09.960
But some browsers still support, uh,
independent text size, uh, zooming itself

00:07:14.526 --> 00:07:16.464
Firefox still ships with this today.

00:07:16.873 --> 00:07:22.417
So Firefox has zoom text only in their
settings, and it does exactly that.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:23.866
It zooms the text.

00:07:24.308 --> 00:07:28.667
Uh, the viewport width stays
fixed, only the text scales up.

00:07:29.372 --> 00:07:32.049
Same reflow pressure as narrow screens.

00:07:32.134 --> 00:07:37.394
So what, what that means is when the
text scales up but the, but the layout

00:07:37.405 --> 00:07:44.693
doesn't, it, it kind of, uh, implements
like a responsive reflow ne-necessity.

00:07:44.893 --> 00:07:53.411
And if your website was not coded
to, uh, reflow text properly, um,

00:07:53.677 --> 00:07:56.675
regardless of the viewport size,
then you could definitely run

00:07:56.675 --> 00:07:58.981
into some, some zoom issues here.

00:08:00.557 --> 00:08:06.633
These are generally different, uh, stress
tests than, uh… Or this is generally a

00:08:06.633 --> 00:08:08.733
different stress test than full page zoom.

00:08:08.948 --> 00:08:13.941
So full page zoom and text only
zoom are really, are two separate,

00:08:14.063 --> 00:08:18.940
uh, types of evaluation, you know,
because the full page zoom zooms

00:08:18.943 --> 00:08:23.474
everything and the text only holds
the width and only grows the text.

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:28.547
This, this is kind of the rub that
I had on the remediation side with

00:08:28.549 --> 00:08:33.389
my team and, and, and that brought
a lot of questions to mind, like,

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:35.140
well, what's really required?

00:08:35.652 --> 00:08:40.429
Um, it's really h- it's kinda hard from
a technical standpoint to, to make both

00:08:40.432 --> 00:08:43.982
of these align in every given scenario

00:08:49.016 --> 00:08:54.256
So I wanna touch a little bit on the exact
language of the success criteria here.

00:08:54.425 --> 00:09:00.843
WCAG 2.2 Understanding Success
Criteria 1.4.4, and I'm gonna

00:09:00.843 --> 00:09:02.967
read this, uh, verbatim.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:10.021
"Content satisfies the criterion if
it can be scaled up to 200% using

00:09:10.021 --> 00:09:15.172
at least one text-scaling mechanis-
mechanism supported by user agents."

00:09:16.684 --> 00:09:19.284
So full page zoom is one mechanism.

00:09:19.898 --> 00:09:22.853
Text only resize is another mechanism.

00:09:23.807 --> 00:09:27.986
This creates a real tension, and
it's worth, like, sitting with, and

00:09:27.986 --> 00:09:29.704
it's worth your team evaluating.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:38.703
So, uh, passing on a full page zoom alone
is technically enough, but then we get

00:09:38.721 --> 00:09:44.985
issues from auditors on the remediation
side that are text, text only zoom issues.

00:09:44.995 --> 00:09:48.761
So this creates a confusion,
and this creates ambiguity.

00:09:49.509 --> 00:09:53.066
And sometimes it's generally a
judgment call when text only mode

00:09:53.077 --> 00:09:56.004
breaks it, uh, but zoom passes.

00:09:56.689 --> 00:10:02.452
This is… Like I said, this is the exact,
uh, ambiguity that causes inconsistency

00:10:02.459 --> 00:10:05.605
in flagging, uh, zoom, uh, issues.

00:10:06.496 --> 00:10:12.283
So the, the overarching answer
here is that there is no

00:10:12.284 --> 00:10:14.249
universal answer to this problem.

00:10:15.035 --> 00:10:21.461
The criterion just, uh, requests that
at least one of these mechanisms pass.

00:10:22.461 --> 00:10:29.151
It's up to you and your team to come
up with a process in deciding if one

00:10:29.673 --> 00:10:32.923
needs to pass, if the other needs
to pass, or if both need to pass

00:10:35.774 --> 00:10:38.735
So where exactly do I land on this, right?

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:44.582
I kinda like, uh, these kinda
gray areas where the criterion

00:10:44.585 --> 00:10:49.010
actually allows us to kinda lean
in and make our own judgment calls.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:56.122
In- inconsistent flagging on my own, o-
my own team pushed me to define this REAL

00:10:56.124 --> 00:11:03.963
process And this is kind of, uh, point
by point exactly where I kinda land.

00:11:04.627 --> 00:11:09.990
Full page zoom plus reflow, the
primary baseline for every audit.

00:11:10.153 --> 00:11:16.527
So this is, uh, zoom the whole page
up, and this is what we should be

00:11:16.527 --> 00:11:18.771
using as our default for, for passing.

00:11:19.546 --> 00:11:24.539
Text-only resize testing,
supplemental but a best practice,

00:11:24.845 --> 00:11:27.589
not an automatic double A failure.

00:11:29.266 --> 00:11:34.165
Now we will weight text-only resize
a little more heavily if this is a

00:11:34.183 --> 00:11:40.414
triple A audit and remediation or
higher risk audit where, uh, compliance

00:11:40.427 --> 00:11:45.873
is, is of the utmost and like, such
as government or higher education.

00:11:47.466 --> 00:11:48.212
Severity.

00:11:49.643 --> 00:11:53.557
We decided to label this as a best
practice unless it causes severe

00:11:53.559 --> 00:11:56.038
loss of content or functionality.

00:11:57.013 --> 00:12:00.820
So in summary, what we're doing is
we're leading with the full page zoom,

00:12:01.354 --> 00:12:06.025
but we're following that up with the
text-only zoom to ensure that we're

00:12:06.025 --> 00:12:12.564
not… there isn't complete loss of
functionality with the text-only zoom.

00:12:13.391 --> 00:12:15.807
So this isn't just one success criteria.

00:12:16.035 --> 00:12:20.086
This is kind of a whole landscape
of success criteria when it

00:12:20.086 --> 00:12:21.778
comes to evaluating zoom.

00:12:22.571 --> 00:12:24.886
Now I won't go into super
detail about all of these.

00:12:24.918 --> 00:12:29.627
I will read them out for, for
those listening, um, real quick.

00:12:29.644 --> 00:12:42.153
But success criterion 1.4.4 Resize
Text, uh, 1.4.10 Reflow, 1.4.12 Text

00:12:42.165 --> 00:12:47.844
Spacing, 1.4.8 Visual Presentation.

00:12:49.233 --> 00:12:54.527
But I wanna take a little bit of
a, a, a deeper dive on a couple of

00:12:54.528 --> 00:13:02.663
these that are, uh, uh, pertinent
to zoom, and that being 1.4.4 Resize

00:13:02.702 --> 00:13:05.852
Text which is a double A level.

00:13:06.817 --> 00:13:11.771
Text can be resized without assistive
technology up to 200% without

00:13:11.783 --> 00:13:14.150
loss of content or functionality.

00:13:15.046 --> 00:13:20.989
So this applies to text but in
practice, practice tested via page zoom.

00:13:22.074 --> 00:13:27.878
We, we don't want, uh, horizontal
scrolling, uh, to, to be required for

00:13:27.878 --> 00:13:33.940
reading text, and there are exceptions,
captions or images with text on them.

00:13:34.382 --> 00:13:40.803
The next criterion is 1.4.10
Reflow, which is a AA level.

00:13:41.574 --> 00:13:45.591
Content can be presented without
loss of information or functionality

00:13:45.605 --> 00:13:51.949
and without requiring scrolling in
two dimensions at 400% zoom or a

00:13:51.949 --> 00:13:56.152
viewport width of 320 CSS pixels.

00:13:57.984 --> 00:14:00.531
So the key phrase is without
scrolling in two dimensions.

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:02.492
So what are dimensions, right?

00:14:02.584 --> 00:14:07.046
Uh, you know, they're typically
horizontal and vertical scrolling.

00:14:07.425 --> 00:14:12.257
Vertical scrolling is always
fine, uh, in, in most regards.

00:14:12.704 --> 00:14:17.012
Um, it's-- we gotta be mindful
of the, if horizontal scrolling

00:14:17.045 --> 00:14:20.089
is required for, uh, readability.

00:14:21.094 --> 00:14:24.041
And the reason why the success
criterion kind of touches on four

00:14:24.041 --> 00:14:30.050
hundred percent zoom or three hundred
and twenty CSS pixels is because,

00:14:30.185 --> 00:14:35.314
uh, at the r- at the right viewport,
these are essentially the same thing.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:42.351
So, uh, we're gonna touch on that here in
a little bit on what that, uh, baseline,

00:14:42.492 --> 00:14:48.115
uh, um, viewport width is and kinda go
through kinda some of the math breakdown

00:14:48.142 --> 00:14:53.445
and what can actually lead to some
in-inconsistencies if, uh, screen size

00:14:53.449 --> 00:14:55.604
is not appropriately set when testing.

00:14:58.393 --> 00:15:03.125
So 1.4.10, uh, has exceptions.

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:08.252
So n- um, content that is inherently
two-dimensional is exempt.

00:15:08.262 --> 00:15:10.565
So what is content that
is two-dimensional, right?

00:15:11.912 --> 00:15:17.199
Data tables, uh, toolbars and menus
that require 2D layout to remain

00:15:17.205 --> 00:15:22.873
usable, maps, diagrams, images
requiring 2D layout to understand

00:15:25.598 --> 00:15:29.498
We have to be careful with exceptions too,
because exceptions can be over applied.

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:31.989
We don't wanna under apply them,
we don't wanna over apply them.

00:15:31.991 --> 00:15:35.610
We wanna, we wanna, uh,
have appropriate exceptions.

00:15:36.528 --> 00:15:42.364
Using a, a, a table, uh, element al-
alone doesn't grant an exemption.

00:15:43.141 --> 00:15:47.687
Um, the exception is generally
for tabular relational content.

00:15:48.398 --> 00:15:53.556
I'll kinda hit on this exact case
in our pricing table example later.

00:15:54.944 --> 00:15:58.584
But before we do that, I kinda wanna
touch on a few misconceptions before

00:15:58.584 --> 00:16:03.617
we get to kind of a framework for
evaluating zoom appro- appropriately.

00:16:04.656 --> 00:16:08.598
So misconception one: if it
looks broken, it fails WCAG.

00:16:09.536 --> 00:16:14.196
Visual imperfection does not
equal accessibility barrier.

00:16:14.226 --> 00:16:19.054
This is typically over-flagging, and
this is kind of some of the stuff that

00:16:19.055 --> 00:16:23.802
I saw kind of bubbling up through our,
uh, remediate- remediation projects

00:16:23.802 --> 00:16:32.436
were, um, uh, with, with 200 or 400%
zoom, things didn't look great, right?

00:16:32.449 --> 00:16:38.222
Like, now it was still readable, but it
didn't l- you know, it may have overflowed

00:16:38.228 --> 00:16:45.488
a container or, uh, um, or overflowed
an image ever so slightly, but the text

00:16:45.488 --> 00:16:50.511
was still readable, and these, these are
visual imperfections, and they should

00:16:50.521 --> 00:16:56.009
be flagged as, uh, visual notes rather
than strict accessibility barriers.

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:59.343
And what… And, you know, from the
remediation side, why does my team

00:16:59.346 --> 00:17:00.841
push back on these things, right?

00:17:00.873 --> 00:17:05.056
Because we have clients that
are, are… that have remediation

00:17:05.056 --> 00:17:09.349
budgets, and we wanna be mindful of
those budgets that we are, we are

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:14.665
focusing on all the accessibility,
um, the strict accessibility

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:19.031
violations, and we're utilizing
that client's budget appropriately

00:17:19.045 --> 00:17:20.881
for the appropriate remediation.

00:17:21.074 --> 00:17:24.927
Reflow isn't just about horiz-
horizontal scroll bars either.

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:28.410
This can lead to under-flagging
if all you're looking for are

00:17:28.423 --> 00:17:30.637
horizontal scroll bars, right?

00:17:31.117 --> 00:17:33.782
There, uh, uh, there could be overlap.

00:17:33.851 --> 00:17:35.434
There could be hidden content.

00:17:35.452 --> 00:17:39.068
There could be un-breachable
controls or broken focus.

00:17:40.014 --> 00:17:43.528
Um, both of these directions,
the over-flagging and the

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:46.775
under-flagging, uh, can actually be…

00:17:47.485 --> 00:17:57.378
lead to, um, bad or, um, uh, noisy kind
of audit reports to the remediation

00:17:57.382 --> 00:18:00.906
team that the remediation team then
ha- has to question and push back,

00:18:01.342 --> 00:18:04.920
and this all takes time, and like I
said, being mindful of that client's

00:18:04.926 --> 00:18:07.496
remediation budget is, is important.

00:18:10.579 --> 00:18:16.170
Misconception number two, 400%
zoom always means the same setup.

00:18:16.327 --> 00:18:20.755
So this is what I kind of alluded
to a couple slides ago around, uh,

00:18:20.765 --> 00:18:22.542
having the appropriate viewport.

00:18:23.126 --> 00:18:27.186
So let's dive in a little bit to the,
the math behind this and make sure that

00:18:27.206 --> 00:18:32.272
we are setting up our, our computer
screens and our m- our browser screens,

00:18:32.339 --> 00:18:35.164
um, appropriately for zoom testing.

00:18:36.246 --> 00:18:40.126
So 1280 pixel window at 400% zoom

00:18:42.651 --> 00:18:47.055
equals a 320 pixel effective width.

00:18:47.168 --> 00:18:52.489
This is the benchmark that the su-
success criterion is, is, is built off of.

00:18:54.064 --> 00:19:01.045
And, and so the 1,280 pixels at 400%
is effectively the same thing as

00:19:01.047 --> 00:19:03.695
setting your viewport to 320 pixels.

00:19:03.995 --> 00:19:10.562
It will enact the same, uh,
responsive like, uh, triggers.

00:19:12.419 --> 00:19:18.397
Now if we look at a screen that's
set to a 1920 pixel window viewport

00:19:18.486 --> 00:19:25.276
at 400% zoom, this ends up with
an effective width of 480 pixels.

00:19:26.946 --> 00:19:31.942
So this actually goes outside of the
benchmark that the success criterion

00:19:31.946 --> 00:19:37.216
set for us for testing, and this
is generally just false, uh, false

00:19:37.225 --> 00:19:43.021
positives waiting to happen because we
don't have consistency between our, our

00:19:43.022 --> 00:19:45.569
browser setup for different auditors.

00:19:47.361 --> 00:19:52.443
The fix here is set DevTools
responsive mode to 320 pixels directly.

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:56.675
Don't always just trust
the zoom percentage alone.

00:19:57.993 --> 00:20:02.610
And finally, misconception three,
it's a data table, so it's exempt.

00:20:04.411 --> 00:20:06.956
The test isn't does it look like a table?

00:20:07.074 --> 00:20:11.286
It's is the row-column relationship
essential to understanding it?

00:20:13.043 --> 00:20:18.152
Exempt, a real data table where
reading across and down together

00:20:18.164 --> 00:20:20.425
is what gives a number meaning.

00:20:20.976 --> 00:20:25.675
Not exempt, a pricing table with
independent self-contained columns

00:20:26.062 --> 00:20:27.788
just laid out side by side.

00:20:29.297 --> 00:20:31.799
So you always have to use this evaluation.

00:20:31.903 --> 00:20:37.980
I- is it, is it, is it technically
a data table, not does it just

00:20:38.141 --> 00:20:40.696
visually look like a data table?

00:20:44.378 --> 00:20:45.427
So where is the line?

00:20:46.283 --> 00:20:48.202
Not every Zoom quirk is equal.

00:20:48.815 --> 00:20:53.516
We need a way to sort visual
imperfection, a usability concern,

00:20:53.917 --> 00:20:55.504
and accessibility barriers.

00:20:56.403 --> 00:21:00.897
And this is where my, my team stepped
back and we evaluated this, and we asked

00:21:00.910 --> 00:21:06.050
the whole team for input on, on what we,
how we thought these things should fall.

00:21:06.506 --> 00:21:10.873
And we kinda came up with this
three-tiered framework for evaluating Zoom

00:21:15.936 --> 00:21:18.127
Tier one, visual imperfection.

00:21:18.421 --> 00:21:23.331
Looks off, but all content and
functionality remains available

00:21:23.332 --> 00:21:25.406
and usable, use- usable.

00:21:26.754 --> 00:21:28.802
This is not a WCAG failure.

00:21:29.446 --> 00:21:34.553
Examples of this are like line
breaks, uh, white space, icon sizing.

00:21:35.341 --> 00:21:37.949
Nothing lost, nothing blocked.

00:21:38.487 --> 00:21:43.820
We log this as design polish,
if at all, and we don't cite

00:21:43.832 --> 00:21:45.275
any success criteria here

00:21:47.858 --> 00:21:50.783
Tier two, usability concern.

00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:56.323
Harder to use, more effortful,
but content and functionality

00:21:56.362 --> 00:21:57.893
technically still reachable.

00:21:58.714 --> 00:22:03.237
Often not a strict accessibility
failure, but sometimes we flag it.

00:22:03.614 --> 00:22:08.655
Examples of these, long line lengths,
you know, hard to hit controls.

00:22:09.485 --> 00:22:13.473
So this may, this may even
implicate other success criteria

00:22:13.485 --> 00:22:15.639
such as target size separately.

00:22:16.477 --> 00:22:22.140
This is kind of the gray area, uh, where
sometimes professionals disagree, and

00:22:22.143 --> 00:22:24.792
sometimes judgment calls are the norm

00:22:27.518 --> 00:22:29.471
Tier three accessibility barrier.

00:22:29.971 --> 00:22:33.230
Content or functionality is
lost, hidden, or unreachable.

00:22:33.853 --> 00:22:37.549
Two-dimensional scrolling is required
outside of a valid exception.

00:22:38.265 --> 00:22:41.119
This is a WCAG failure.

00:22:42.858 --> 00:22:45.337
Cite the ex- the success criteria.

00:22:45.348 --> 00:22:50.821
So this-- So examples of this is,
like, clipped text, uh, o- overlapped,

00:22:50.905 --> 00:22:56.350
blocked controls, uh, forced
horizontal constr- uh, uh, scroll.

00:22:56.382 --> 00:23:01.235
So these are where you would
actually flag these as failures

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:08.523
Now, so now that we have our framework
in place, we kinda understand the

00:23:08.524 --> 00:23:11.550
misconceptions, we kinda understand
some of the tooling around the

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:13.513
correct setup for testing Zoom.

00:23:14.442 --> 00:23:19.048
Now let's take all that and let's look at
some real world examples and walk through

00:23:19.049 --> 00:23:24.511
some real patterns, and we'll sort each
of these into the correct framework tier

00:23:28.608 --> 00:23:32.227
So the first example here
is a sticky header overlap.

00:23:32.253 --> 00:23:35.484
I actually have images here,
but I'm gonna do my best to read

00:23:35.484 --> 00:23:37.363
these out for those listening.

00:23:38.144 --> 00:23:42.288
A fixed header stays pinned
while the page zooms to 400%.

00:23:43.157 --> 00:23:47.766
Zoomed in content scrolls
underneath it and becomes hidden.

00:23:48.797 --> 00:23:52.646
So we would flag this as a tier
three accessibility barrier.

00:23:53.035 --> 00:23:56.125
This is a lot of times one
of the most common WordPress

00:23:56.149 --> 00:23:58.331
reflow failures that we see.

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:04.827
Um, you know, content forms, uh,
form, form fields, links can end

00:24:04.829 --> 00:24:09.696
up hidden behind this header and
can cause, like, a loss of content.

00:24:10.447 --> 00:24:17.015
And, uh, uh, this is common with, uh, you
know, page builders or WordPress themes

00:24:17.015 --> 00:24:22.367
that have hard-coded header heights built
into them, and where from a development

00:24:22.367 --> 00:24:25.943
side, a lot of times you wanna ensure that
you're not putting those fixed heights

00:24:25.956 --> 00:24:28.503
onto that so that the reflow can happen

00:24:32.091 --> 00:24:33.629
The next example is a mega menu.

00:24:35.308 --> 00:24:42.125
We have a m-multi-column mega menu that
widens instead of stacking at 400% zoom.

00:24:42.798 --> 00:24:46.351
Reading it requires horizontal
scrolling to see all columns.

00:24:47.274 --> 00:24:51.004
We would set this as a tier
three accessibility barrier.

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:56.485
Uh, developers should take, uh, care
to make sure that these mega menus

00:24:56.528 --> 00:25:01.033
are tested with zoom, and that they
d- they aren't just dependent on

00:25:01.042 --> 00:25:03.440
viewport, uh, responsive scaling.

00:25:04.114 --> 00:25:08.407
So we would co- you should collapse
those into one stacked column.

00:25:09.247 --> 00:25:13.766
Um, this is easily fixable with,
with CSS and easily fixable if we

00:25:13.767 --> 00:25:17.807
properly test, uh, 200 and 400 zoom.

00:25:22.558 --> 00:25:24.572
Truncated button label.

00:25:26.273 --> 00:25:28.553
A CTA button has a fixed width.

00:25:29.303 --> 00:25:36.158
At 200% zoom, Schedule a consultation
becomes Schedule a con with an ellipses,

00:25:37.304 --> 00:25:39.407
and the full label is never revealed.

00:25:41.051 --> 00:25:44.212
I'd label this as a tier
three accessibility barrier

00:25:44.290 --> 00:25:45.829
because of the loss of content.

00:25:47.039 --> 00:25:51.736
So a, a fixed width plus an ellipses
is a very common thing that we see

00:25:51.736 --> 00:25:56.483
in page builders and, and, you know,
they've gotten better, but in, in

00:25:56.483 --> 00:25:58.139
the past we saw this pattern a lot.

00:25:59.341 --> 00:26:03.101
The, the meaning is lost for the
sighted user, even if there is a

00:26:03.114 --> 00:26:06.735
proper Aria label, uh, being applied
here to where the screen reader

00:26:06.745 --> 00:26:08.448
will still get the full context.

00:26:09.089 --> 00:26:14.697
But in this case, I would still flag
this as a, a, a failure due to the,

00:26:14.779 --> 00:26:16.824
the loss of content for the visual user

00:26:19.805 --> 00:26:24.855
An interesting kind of aside here is
a lot of times this implementation of

00:26:24.877 --> 00:26:32.204
truncating text is not always, uh, easily
found by automated scanners, such, such

00:26:32.205 --> 00:26:34.986
as our accessibility checker plugin.

00:26:35.662 --> 00:26:40.958
And, um, and this creates a, a
little bit of a rub there, so it's…

00:26:40.973 --> 00:26:44.772
because it's harder for our automated
tools to catch these things, and it

00:26:44.772 --> 00:26:50.154
requires that manual audit to, to,
to do a visual check to catch these.

00:26:53.337 --> 00:26:55.368
So in the next example, overlapping text.

00:26:55.420 --> 00:26:59.148
A hero heading and a subheading
use absolute positioning.

00:26:59.965 --> 00:27:04.103
At four hundred percent, the subheading
slides up and overlaps the, the

00:27:04.103 --> 00:27:07.134
heading and both become unreadable.

00:27:08.158 --> 00:27:11.136
I would flag this as a Tier
3 accessibility barrier.

00:27:12.602 --> 00:27:16.386
It's a common problem in WordPress
themes where y- you know, on subpages

00:27:16.389 --> 00:27:21.185
you have these, these, these headers
that, uh, that, you know, we want

00:27:21.189 --> 00:27:25.157
to align the, the heading and the
subheading to be, like, in the middle.

00:27:25.494 --> 00:27:29.777
And, um, you know, we're using kind
of more traditional CSS practices

00:27:29.779 --> 00:27:36.121
here and absolute, uh, position-
positioning, relative font sizing and

00:27:36.363 --> 00:27:38.439
not testing it past a hundred percent.

00:27:38.507 --> 00:27:42.156
Um, normally this is f- still
fine for a screen reader.

00:27:42.158 --> 00:27:45.310
A screen reader is still gonna
read this out, but it's completely

00:27:45.350 --> 00:27:50.560
unreadable for, uh, sighted, uh,
users that are, um, using zoom.

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:55.519
So developers, we need to take
care that we're not, um, creating

00:27:55.582 --> 00:27:57.832
fragile CSS in this case.

00:28:01.658 --> 00:28:05.606
In the next example, we have a
testimony, testimonial carousel.

00:28:05.818 --> 00:28:12.137
A fixed height carousel slide clips, uh,
longer testimonial text at 200% zoom.

00:28:12.829 --> 00:28:15.058
The end of the quote is simply cut off.

00:28:15.142 --> 00:28:19.476
No scroll bar, no indication
more test, text exists.

00:28:20.224 --> 00:28:23.138
So we call this a tier
three accessibility barrier.

00:28:23.144 --> 00:28:27.220
Fixed height containers are,
are a recurring root cause with

00:28:27.567 --> 00:28:31.998
sliders that utilize JavaScript,
and a lot of times they just…

00:28:32.082 --> 00:28:36.745
They, uh, they assume the height at
100% zoom, and they never check the

00:28:36.745 --> 00:28:40.657
200 and 400 zoom, uh, requirement.

00:28:41.331 --> 00:28:44.745
In this case, there's no read more,
there's no scroll bar, so it's

00:28:44.745 --> 00:28:48.405
hard to, it's hard to know that
there actually is more text below

00:28:53.214 --> 00:28:57.021
Now we have the, the pricing table
example that we keep referring back to.

00:28:57.147 --> 00:29:01.893
We have a four-column pricing
table that doesn't reflow at 400%

00:29:01.999 --> 00:29:03.940
and requires horizontal scrolling.

00:29:04.664 --> 00:29:07.713
Client argues it's a table, it's exempt.

00:29:08.544 --> 00:29:10.275
But I would question, is it?

00:29:12.013 --> 00:29:18.374
If the row-column relationship is
essential or just, uh, or j- is the

00:29:18.374 --> 00:29:23.053
row-column relationship essential or
is this just a grid design choice?

00:29:23.105 --> 00:29:26.481
Are they just using the
table for layout alone?

00:29:27.378 --> 00:29:32.069
And most pricing tables that I
come across can, can stack, uh,

00:29:33.294 --> 00:29:37.742
ca- can be stacked and they are
not relat- relational, uh, tables.

00:29:38.166 --> 00:29:42.617
So in this case, flag this
as a tier three not exempt

00:29:45.633 --> 00:29:48.276
In the next example,
we have uneven spacing.

00:29:48.431 --> 00:29:53.980
At 200% zoom, paragraph spacing looks
slightly inconsistent between sections.

00:29:54.587 --> 00:29:58.436
Nothing is clipped, nothing is
unreachable, everything reads fine.

00:29:59.556 --> 00:30:02.646
So I would flag this as a
tier one visual imperfection.

00:30:02.828 --> 00:30:09.030
And while things don't always look perfect
at, at these zoom levels, they are just

00:30:09.030 --> 00:30:15.283
that, visual imperfections that are design
notes, not hard accessibility failures.

00:30:15.711 --> 00:30:18.794
So in this example, we have
shrinking icon buttons.

00:30:19.453 --> 00:30:24.540
An icon-only button shrinks
proportionally at 400% zoom.

00:30:25.269 --> 00:30:30.115
Still tappable, still labeled via
an ARIA label, but now closer to

00:30:30.117 --> 00:30:32.023
the minimal comfortable target size.

00:30:33.501 --> 00:30:37.139
I'd label this as a tier two
usability concern, and there may be

00:30:37.149 --> 00:30:41.910
implications for other success criteria
here as well, such as target size.

00:30:42.409 --> 00:30:47.124
But this highlights the gray zone examples
that we've kind of been bringing here.

00:30:47.444 --> 00:30:52.232
Label and functionality are both still
present, but you may need to check

00:30:52.241 --> 00:30:55.068
against some other criteria in this case

00:30:58.656 --> 00:31:02.483
So in this final example here,
we have text-only zoom overlap.

00:31:03.629 --> 00:31:09.208
A card component passes full
page zoom cleanly at 400%.

00:31:09.648 --> 00:31:13.437
Enable Firefox's zoom text only instead.

00:31:14.101 --> 00:31:18.981
The heading grows, the width doesn't,
and it overlaps the card's body text.

00:31:19.415 --> 00:31:23.776
This is either a tier two or a
tier three, depending on severity.

00:31:25.471 --> 00:31:29.871
So this is kind of where the rub
is with, with testing for zoom.

00:31:30.314 --> 00:31:35.522
We call back to the success
criteria, the 1.4.4, and, and it

00:31:35.523 --> 00:31:37.735
requires at least one mechanism.

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:42.041
Well, this passes with, with
full page zoom, but it doesn't

00:31:42.043 --> 00:31:44.349
pass with text-only zoom.

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:51.148
So it's… technically this could
be a pass, um, but you could go the

00:31:51.149 --> 00:31:57.632
extra mile and do a supplemental
issue, uh, if for higher tier, uh,

00:31:58.155 --> 00:32:01.879
AAA requirement, uh, evaluations.

00:32:02.349 --> 00:32:06.516
Or if you put this off towards the end
of the project and there's some time left

00:32:06.519 --> 00:32:13.143
over, you've solved all of the critical,
um, the higher severity issues, and you

00:32:13.143 --> 00:32:16.858
have some time left over, maybe this
is a good time to take that secondary

00:32:16.859 --> 00:32:22.144
pass on the text-only zoom and see if
we can improve this and make it better.

00:32:22.661 --> 00:32:28.723
But on its face, it's not a straight
hard accessibility failure because it

00:32:28.797 --> 00:32:31.475
passes at least one of the mechanisms.

00:32:34.234 --> 00:32:36.193
So how do we fix these things, right?

00:32:36.393 --> 00:32:43.009
So in, in the, in, in the button example,
I'm gonna dive into some CSS here and

00:32:43.010 --> 00:32:47.000
go through this line by line to help
the, those listening, um, understand.

00:32:48.728 --> 00:32:54.645
So we have a button, and typically,
uh, buttons, uh, can be coded with

00:32:54.648 --> 00:33:01.742
some fixed widths and some hard, uh,
uh, text units, font sizing units.

00:33:03.065 --> 00:33:06.868
So at the top here, we have
some CSS that breaks under zoom.

00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:13.946
I have a button class with a width
set to 220 pixels, overflow hidden,

00:33:14.529 --> 00:33:17.831
and text overflow set to ellipses.

00:33:18.337 --> 00:33:21.650
And this creates that… the
example that we showed with the,

00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:23.693
uh, the, the button earlier.

00:33:24.552 --> 00:33:27.358
But we actually want
it to survive zooming.

00:33:27.526 --> 00:33:29.891
So how can we change the CSS?

00:33:31.572 --> 00:33:38.179
We take that button class, and we change
it to have these CSS properties, a

00:33:38.179 --> 00:33:45.532
minimum width of 220 pixels, a width set
to max content, and a max width of 100%.

00:33:46.151 --> 00:33:51.181
So now when the page is zoomed, the
button text will reflow properly

00:33:55.528 --> 00:33:56.996
Now we wanna fix grids.

00:33:57.339 --> 00:34:03.257
And, uh, so in some, in the, in
the mega menu and the pricing table

00:34:03.257 --> 00:34:08.647
example that we showed earlier, it
had-- it used grid template columns

00:34:08.813 --> 00:34:10.777
set somewhat similar to this.

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:16.875
Grid template column with a
repeat of three and a min-max

00:34:16.951 --> 00:34:19.103
of zero and one fraction.

00:34:20.147 --> 00:34:24.940
Now, this is, this is gonna cause issues
when you go to zoom because it will

00:34:24.940 --> 00:34:28.254
not, uh, reflow and scale appropriately.

00:34:28.971 --> 00:34:33.813
So we can fix that CSS, that one line
of CSS here, by setting grid template

00:34:33.815 --> 00:34:41.233
column repeat to auto-fit with a
min-max of 18ems and one fraction.

00:34:42.839 --> 00:34:48.480
So it's a common misconception that grid
support alone guarantees that things

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:52.788
will be responsive and that, that,
that they will, uh, properly size to

00:34:52.791 --> 00:34:55.679
zoom, but that's not always the case.

00:34:55.686 --> 00:35:00.294
We need to take, uh, uh, measures to
ensure that we are not using fixed

00:35:00.294 --> 00:35:02.661
widths and absolute font sizes.

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:06.322
So how to actually test this?

00:35:07.729 --> 00:35:12.779
So zoom the real browser using Control
or Command+, not just dev tools.

00:35:13.182 --> 00:35:18.453
Also verify effective width
matches 320 pixels at 400%.

00:35:19.632 --> 00:35:23.505
Test with your keyboard focus moving
through the page, not just visually

00:35:23.506 --> 00:35:29.595
scanning, because things can become broken
and can be obscured for keyboard users.

00:35:30.579 --> 00:35:33.308
Check both 200% and 400%.

00:35:33.465 --> 00:35:34.867
They can fail independently.

00:35:35.296 --> 00:35:40.156
And optionally toggle on Firefox's
zoom text only as a supplemental

00:35:40.158 --> 00:35:41.880
check for higher risk audits.

00:35:44.455 --> 00:35:46.089
So why do teams disagree on this?

00:35:46.607 --> 00:35:48.377
I've watched this happen on my own team.

00:35:48.645 --> 00:35:49.960
That's what started this talk.

00:35:50.624 --> 00:35:53.471
Um, this could be because
of inconsistent test setup.

00:35:53.569 --> 00:35:57.301
Make sure that everybody's testing
at the appropriate viewport widths.

00:35:57.882 --> 00:36:02.558
Um, no shared vocabulary for
imperfection versus barrier.

00:36:03.413 --> 00:36:06.596
No shared policy on which
zoom mechanism even counts.

00:36:06.609 --> 00:36:09.915
Are we using full page zoom or
are we using text only zoom?

00:36:10.375 --> 00:36:11.466
Define it for your team.

00:36:12.127 --> 00:36:16.343
Pressure to either over
flag, uh, or under flag

00:36:20.734 --> 00:36:25.201
So let's pause here for the last, uh,
few minutes of this talk and kinda

00:36:25.212 --> 00:36:28.551
do a recap on the framework, 'cause
these are, these are things that you

00:36:28.551 --> 00:36:33.386
wanna ask when you are evaluating your,
your websites for zoom accessibility.

00:36:34.013 --> 00:36:39.341
One, is any content or functionality
lost, hidden, or unreachable?

00:36:39.944 --> 00:36:44.944
Two, is two-dimensional scrolling
required outside of a valid exception?

00:36:45.551 --> 00:36:49.218
Three, if neither, is
it still hard to use?

00:36:49.699 --> 00:36:53.181
Log it as a usability
i- issue, not a failure.

00:36:53.924 --> 00:36:57.776
Otherwise, it's a visual,
visual note, not a finding.

00:36:58.846 --> 00:37:03.196
So l- the key takeaways here are
that WCAG's zoom requirements are

00:37:03.199 --> 00:37:10.109
specific, 200% text resize, 400% at
320 pixels reflow, and, and test those.

00:37:10.145 --> 00:37:10.911
They're testable.

00:37:11.915 --> 00:37:16.529
Most zoom bugs trace back to fixed
dimensions and absolute positioning.

00:37:16.556 --> 00:37:21.560
Developers, take care that you're
not using these fixed, um, CSS

00:37:21.582 --> 00:37:27.736
properties so that, um, zoom and
viewport scaling works naturally.

00:37:29.013 --> 00:37:32.854
The spec is genuinely ambiguous in places.

00:37:33.234 --> 00:37:35.836
Don't leave that to a
case-by-case judgment call.

00:37:36.107 --> 00:37:38.009
Define your team's standards

00:37:42.234 --> 00:37:45.917
And finally, I would just implore you
to go a little bit deeper on this.

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:51.811
Uh, re-read the success criterion
yourself, specifically 1.4.4

00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:59.999
Resize te- Resize Text, 1.4.10
Reflow, 1.4.12 Text Spacing.

00:38:01.132 --> 00:38:04.767
I hope that this talk has given you,
uh, a little bit more of a background

00:38:04.788 --> 00:38:08.564
on some of the ambiguity here and what
your team can do to define your own

00:38:08.566 --> 00:38:13.118
processes in regard to accessibility
testing and, uh, accessibility

00:38:13.124 --> 00:38:15.501
zoom testing and remediation.

00:38:16.330 --> 00:38:20.604
And, and it implores you to
dive deeper into this subject.

00:38:22.796 --> 00:38:27.529
Again, I want to thank the organizers
of, of the WP Ac- Accessibility

00:38:27.531 --> 00:38:29.841
Conference for having me here today.

00:38:30.458 --> 00:38:38.626
Uh, I, you can find me at, on X or GitHub
or LinkedIn or a lot of other places with,

00:38:38.871 --> 00:38:42.065
with the handles stevejonesdev, no spaces.

00:38:43.682 --> 00:38:48.132
My company, Equalize Digital,
provides, uh, many free educational

00:38:48.144 --> 00:38:50.320
resources related to accessibility.

00:38:50.338 --> 00:38:55.052
You can find those at, at,
uh, our, information about our

00:38:55.729 --> 00:39:00.142
WordPress Accessibility Meetup
at equalizedigital.com/events.

00:39:00.831 --> 00:39:06.575
We have a Facebook group at
facebook.com/groups/wordpress.accessibility.

00:39:08.591 --> 00:39:13.695
And finally, you can listen to our
podcast at accessibilitycraft.com.

00:39:14.399 --> 00:39:17.233
And with that, I'll hand it off for Q&A.

00:39:30.808 --> 00:39:33.075
Hello, hello, everybody.

00:39:33.604 --> 00:39:35.435
Steve, wonderful presentation.

00:39:35.562 --> 00:39:36.808
I didn't even get a preview.

00:39:36.839 --> 00:39:37.814
I was floored by that.

00:39:37.823 --> 00:39:40.892
I love how you went into the
gray areas of, of accessibility

00:39:40.897 --> 00:39:42.129
testing and remediation.

00:39:42.448 --> 00:39:43.178
Super fun.

00:39:43.683 --> 00:39:47.330
There is also, unsurprisingly,
a ton of questions.

00:39:47.336 --> 00:39:50.750
So let's go, get through these,
as m- many of these as we can.

00:39:51.044 --> 00:39:56.167
Claire asks, "Does anyone actually still
use zoom text only?" What do you think?

00:39:56.952 --> 00:39:58.453
Um, uh, I do.

00:39:58.680 --> 00:40:01.113
Uh, we do.

00:40:01.377 --> 00:40:02.580
Yeah, I think so.

00:40:02.658 --> 00:40:07.080
I think that, um, it's, it's not as
common as it used to be, 'cause it

00:40:07.080 --> 00:40:08.606
actually kinda used to be the standard.

00:40:08.606 --> 00:40:12.924
But, uh, no, I don't think it's n-
nearly as used as it used to be.

00:40:13.223 --> 00:40:16.658
There's only a, a few, a couple
browsers that really support it anymore.

00:40:17.022 --> 00:40:20.910
But I think from, like, an auditing
standpoint, I think we still need to do

00:40:20.912 --> 00:40:25.924
our due diligence to check and make sure
that our websites, uh, zoom and reflow

00:40:25.926 --> 00:40:30.144
properly with text-only zooming Right.

00:40:30.190 --> 00:40:33.786
The text available somewhere, so-
Yep … we have to adapt to it.

00:40:33.866 --> 00:40:36.957
All right, uh, next one also
related to zoom text only.

00:40:37.047 --> 00:40:40.701
Uh, does that only exist in Firefox
as far as you're aware, or is it

00:40:40.701 --> 00:40:42.049
available in other browsers too?

00:40:42.843 --> 00:40:45.226
Uh, I think it's in, in Safari as well.

00:40:45.226 --> 00:40:49.505
So Firefox, Safari, you can get text
only zoom, but most of the Chromium

00:40:49.505 --> 00:40:53.709
browsers like Chrome, Edge, Brave,
they don't support the text only zoom.

00:40:54.819 --> 00:40:55.147
Got it.

00:40:55.794 --> 00:40:59.158
All right, and, uh, next one
from an anonymous attendee.

00:40:59.173 --> 00:41:03.095
As a developer who frequently
oversees remediation on a variety of

00:41:03.096 --> 00:41:08.660
WordPress sites, what are some of the
most common things you see in other

00:41:08.670 --> 00:41:13.481
people's code- Mm-hmm … that directly
hurt zoom or reflow capabilities?

00:41:13.989 --> 00:41:17.575
Yeah, the number one violator I
think is gonna be anything fixed.

00:41:18.065 --> 00:41:21.703
Uh, so fixed pi- pixel
widths, fixed pixel heights.

00:41:22.083 --> 00:41:25.426
You know, like the example I showed
in the talk with the navigation menu.

00:41:25.428 --> 00:41:29.089
A lot of times, you know, page builders
or websites will be coded with a fixed

00:41:29.114 --> 00:41:34.077
pixel height on their navigation menu
that kind of fixes to the top of the page.

00:41:34.100 --> 00:41:36.892
And then when reflow
happens, it doesn't happen.

00:41:36.893 --> 00:41:41.136
It either you get horizontal
scrolling or, or, uh, controls such

00:41:41.136 --> 00:41:42.709
as links and things get cut off.

00:41:43.832 --> 00:41:44.855
So, yeah.

00:41:45.053 --> 00:41:45.268
Yeah.

00:41:46.556 --> 00:41:46.993
All right.

00:41:47.031 --> 00:41:49.624
And, uh, William Patton, gee,
that name sounds familiar.

00:41:49.624 --> 00:41:49.636
Yeah.

00:41:49.683 --> 00:41:55.220
Uh, he asks, uh, "When content is
grids, like rows of three or four

00:41:55.227 --> 00:41:58.489
at zoom levels this high without
needing horizontal and vertical

00:41:58.489 --> 00:42:01.482
scrolling to read it?" Not su- Yeah.

00:42:01.850 --> 00:42:02.080
Yeah.

00:42:02.115 --> 00:42:02.450
Yeah.

00:42:02.656 --> 00:42:05.007
I mean, it depends what's
inside those grids, right?

00:42:05.553 --> 00:42:07.875
Like, if you have just an icon
inside that grid, it might fit

00:42:07.877 --> 00:42:09.781
on a smaller viewport size.

00:42:09.810 --> 00:42:13.261
But generally, you wanna collapse
things down into rows on, you

00:42:13.261 --> 00:42:15.881
know, smaller viewports and mobile
devices and things like that.

00:42:15.883 --> 00:42:19.299
I think that's a general rule of
thumb, and it's just, you know,

00:42:19.312 --> 00:42:22.038
outside of accessibility, it's more
pleasant to look at with the eye.

00:42:23.257 --> 00:42:23.491
Cool.

00:42:24.017 --> 00:42:28.236
Uh, and then from an anonymous
attendee, uh, says, "Some, some

00:42:28.237 --> 00:42:31.511
guy named Chris asked…" I mean,
there's not a guy named Chris here.

00:42:31.513 --> 00:42:32.650
I, I don't know who would ask this.

00:42:33.091 --> 00:42:34.723
Some idiot who doesn't know things.

00:42:34.725 --> 00:42:37.469
But, uh- … for the less technical
folks in the audience, can you explain

00:42:37.469 --> 00:42:40.397
exactly, uh, what a CSS pixel is?

00:42:40.906 --> 00:42:41.194
Yeah.

00:42:41.582 --> 00:42:42.767
'Cause that Chris guy doesn't know.

00:42:42.962 --> 00:42:43.868
No, no.

00:42:45.685 --> 00:42:52.011
Well, Chris, whoever you are out there-
… um, uh, CSS pixels is a virtual unit

00:42:52.013 --> 00:42:58.055
of measurement, uh, uh, in, in CSS, and
it's used to define al- almost anything,

00:42:58.113 --> 00:43:01.346
uh, that, uh, gets sized inside of CSS.

00:43:01.371 --> 00:43:05.641
But, uh, I think for the, the
purposes of this talk, I think a lot,

00:43:05.672 --> 00:43:08.019
I'm referring a lot to font sizes.

00:43:08.593 --> 00:43:11.206
Um, but- Mm-hmm … you know, CSS
pixels are used to define the widths

00:43:11.206 --> 00:43:13.371
and heights and, of all kinds of things.

00:43:13.933 --> 00:43:15.627
Um- And is that, like, one-to-one?

00:43:15.629 --> 00:43:16.279
Sorry to cut you off.

00:43:16.281 --> 00:43:18.942
Is that, like, one-to-one to
whatever the monitor's pixel is?

00:43:19.248 --> 00:43:22.921
I mean- Um, or like- Not ex- not exactly.

00:43:22.924 --> 00:43:27.710
I mean, um- No, no, I don't know.

00:43:27.756 --> 00:43:29.431
It, it could be, it couldn't be.

00:43:29.439 --> 00:43:30.690
There's some ambiguity there.

00:43:30.703 --> 00:43:34.005
Sometimes fonts render differently,
sometimes fonts are created differently.

00:43:34.624 --> 00:43:39.944
Um, but the, the, the catch here is that,
you know, sometimes the, the pixel that's

00:43:39.945 --> 00:43:46.577
defined in your, your CSS is not, uh, is
overridden by a user's browser settings.

00:43:47.021 --> 00:43:51.848
And so whether or not your layout
responds to the u- to the user's

00:43:51.863 --> 00:43:57.725
settings and, and zooms that text in
or doesn't zoom that text in, um, can

00:43:57.726 --> 00:44:02.068
be, uh, can create huge implications
for zoom and, and things like that.

00:44:02.068 --> 00:44:07.139
Like, if I go into my browser and
I increase my default font size to,

00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:13.113
like, 18 or 22, uh, and your layout
is, has, you know, absolute units

00:44:13.124 --> 00:44:16.036
and doesn't respond to that, that's
not a great user experience for me.

00:44:17.144 --> 00:44:17.407
Yeah.

00:44:17.581 --> 00:44:17.740
Yeah.

00:44:18.010 --> 00:44:19.807
Well, thank you for
satisfying my curiosity there.

00:44:19.858 --> 00:44:24.751
Uh, so next anonymous attendee asks, "You
pointed out the misconception that testing

00:44:24.784 --> 00:44:32.169
at 400% zoom is always the same as setting
dev tools to a 320 pixel responsive width.

00:44:32.556 --> 00:44:36.532
From a CSS architecture standpoint, what
are the most common modern CSS properties

00:44:36.545 --> 00:44:42.839
or unit choices like vw/vh or, uh, clamp
with parentheses after it that break

00:44:42.841 --> 00:44:46.828
down what, when developers rely solely
on a monitor's browser zoom rather than

00:44:46.828 --> 00:44:50.104
explicit viewport constraints?" Yeah.

00:44:50.153 --> 00:44:50.457
Yeah.

00:44:50.499 --> 00:44:52.870
Uh, I mean, you're getting to the
crux of the problem here, right, from

00:44:52.870 --> 00:44:54.878
a, from a code standpoint, right?

00:44:55.594 --> 00:45:00.045
Um, the biggest issues I see are
just excessive uses of, um, of

00:45:00.054 --> 00:45:01.625
viewport width and viewport height.

00:45:02.237 --> 00:45:05.282
Um, I've seen that applied to
things that I don't think it

00:45:05.282 --> 00:45:07.427
should be applied to or, you know.

00:45:07.427 --> 00:45:11.394
And then, you know, like I said before,
fixed, fixed or minimum widths put on

00:45:11.412 --> 00:45:17.290
things that don't allow, uh, the div to
f- to, to naturally expand so that the

00:45:17.293 --> 00:45:20.359
content inside of it can, can reflow.

00:45:20.492 --> 00:45:25.235
So, like, anything rigid and, and you…
I mean, you mentioned clamp functions.

00:45:25.237 --> 00:45:26.706
Clamp functions are great, right?

00:45:27.071 --> 00:45:32.268
Um, but clamp functions are only
great if they're configured properly.

00:45:32.409 --> 00:45:35.337
So, like, you can put whatever you
want inside that function and tell

00:45:35.337 --> 00:45:39.985
the text to do whatever, whatever you
want it to do, so you still have to

00:45:39.987 --> 00:45:41.728
configure that clamp function properly.

00:45:42.423 --> 00:45:47.034
So I would, I'd recommend sticking
with flexible layouts and relative

00:45:47.034 --> 00:45:51.167
units, meaning that divs can resize.

00:45:51.470 --> 00:45:52.365
There's no fixed width.

00:45:52.415 --> 00:45:56.308
The content inside of them can reflow,
and that font sizes can scale up

00:45:56.309 --> 00:45:58.038
and down based on zoom settings.

00:45:59.229 --> 00:45:59.469
Cool.

00:45:59.758 --> 00:46:01.191
All right, we got three minutes.

00:46:01.248 --> 00:46:05.832
Uh, you discussed the tension between
modern full-page zoom and text-only zoom,

00:46:05.832 --> 00:46:09.301
noting that Firefox ships a zoom text-only
feature that applies reflow pressure

00:46:09.302 --> 00:46:10.744
without scaling the surrounding layout.

00:46:11.061 --> 00:46:13.981
Given this behavior, do you advocate
for WordPress theme developers to

00:46:13.982 --> 00:46:18.714
strictly use REM and EM units for
all structural containers to prevent

00:46:18.728 --> 00:46:22.239
breakage, or do modern layout
techniques like CSS Grid reduce the

00:46:22.239 --> 00:46:23.940
need to test for text-only zoom?

00:46:26.311 --> 00:46:32.241
Well, that last sentence, reduce the
need to test for text-only zoom, I, I,

00:46:32.276 --> 00:46:35.853
I'd caution that, that way of thinking.

00:46:36.346 --> 00:46:40.845
Um, I wouldn't strictly recommend
REMs or EMs for everything.

00:46:40.857 --> 00:46:45.348
Now, I think that your text defined as
REMs or EMs is very good because it's

00:46:45.350 --> 00:46:47.274
gonna naturally scale up and down, right?

00:46:47.944 --> 00:46:50.370
But I don't- I wouldn't use
this as a hard ru- hard rule.

00:46:50.431 --> 00:46:55.536
I actually think that there's many ways
around technically to getting properly

00:46:55.554 --> 00:47:02.490
scaled text without, you know, um, getting
dogmatic, uh, or, you know, absolute

00:47:02.548 --> 00:47:05.514
about the actual units that you use.

00:47:05.536 --> 00:47:10.716
'Cause in the context of what you're
building, a, a pixel may actually be fine,

00:47:10.732 --> 00:47:15.287
and you could actually achieve some things
with a pixel font size correctly, but it's

00:47:15.321 --> 00:47:17.557
all in your implem- your implementation.

00:47:18.269 --> 00:47:23.017
Um, but, you know, Grid, Flexbox, those
things absolutely help with reflow.

00:47:23.018 --> 00:47:24.096
Use those.

00:47:24.413 --> 00:47:27.886
But, but here's where I will,
will disclaim and caution.

00:47:28.528 --> 00:47:34.231
N- no implementation reduces
your need to test for zoom.

00:47:34.683 --> 00:47:38.881
W- would you not test for what
it looks like on a mobile device?

00:47:39.044 --> 00:47:41.337
If, if you're gonna test what it
looks like on a mobile device, you

00:47:41.337 --> 00:47:44.107
need to test what it looks like in
zoom because those two things are

00:47:44.107 --> 00:47:46.009
kind of essentially the same thing.

00:47:46.282 --> 00:47:50.671
And like I said in the talk, zoom is not
necessarily an accessibility feature.

00:47:51.143 --> 00:47:51.804
It's a tool.

00:47:51.854 --> 00:47:53.556
Anybody can use it for any reason.

00:47:55.017 --> 00:47:55.882
So we should support it.

00:47:55.882 --> 00:47:56.040
All right.

00:47:56.576 --> 00:47:56.845
Yep.

00:47:57.082 --> 00:47:58.814
We got a minute 20 seconds.

00:47:58.848 --> 00:47:59.682
Gonna try to get through this.

00:47:59.708 --> 00:48:03.267
Uh, introduced a… You introduced a
three-tier framework to separate visual

00:48:03.268 --> 00:48:04.874
imperfections from usability concerns.

00:48:05.049 --> 00:48:07.790
How do you calibrate your auditing
team to ensure consistent judgment

00:48:07.790 --> 00:48:11.866
calls on tier two usability concerns
where content is more effortful

00:48:12.473 --> 00:48:14.162
but still technically reachable?

00:48:15.769 --> 00:48:18.572
Yeah, so you focus on the
actual user impact, right?

00:48:18.572 --> 00:48:21.864
Like, uh, you know, and that's h- what
you do when you go through the framework.

00:48:21.864 --> 00:48:25.286
You're kind of dec- deciding
what the impact to the user is.

00:48:25.854 --> 00:48:31.817
Um, so, uh, there's a judgment
call when you get to tier two.

00:48:31.915 --> 00:48:35.344
Like, it's not all… It's, that's the
gray, and that's where we're living, and

00:48:35.344 --> 00:48:36.617
that's what this whole talk is about.

00:48:36.846 --> 00:48:38.430
So you have to make a judgment call.

00:48:38.903 --> 00:48:42.705
But it, uh, and a lot of times these
have to be surfaced sometimes to the

00:48:42.705 --> 00:48:45.972
client, you know, 'cause ultimately,
you know, it's their budget.

00:48:46.120 --> 00:48:47.709
They get to decide sometimes.

00:48:48.312 --> 00:48:52.290
Where, where I, where I caution a little
bit, uh, with the auditing team, or where

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:57.184
I actually will push back from remediation
back over to the auditing is where if

00:48:57.184 --> 00:49:02.261
it's a slight visual imperfection and it
just overlays just a little bit on text

00:49:02.327 --> 00:49:05.736
only resize, should we really fix that?

00:49:06.142 --> 00:49:09.560
Like, if it's not really broken
broken, but it looks not super

00:49:09.561 --> 00:49:12.954
great, like is that… Are we being
mindful of the client's budget

00:49:12.964 --> 00:49:15.306
by, by going ahead with that?

00:49:17.631 --> 00:49:18.045
All right.

00:49:18.289 --> 00:49:19.878
Sorry, we gotta stop there, everybody.

00:49:19.893 --> 00:49:22.341
Thank you so much for attending
this session with Steve Jones.

00:49:22.343 --> 00:49:25.514
You can continue the conversation
in Slack or on social media using

